WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.950 --> 00:00:06.680
Four foul-mouthed kids, a ton of oblivious
townspeople, and a whole lot of hidden aliens:

00:00:06.680 --> 00:00:11.940
During its two decades on TV, South Park has
become a cultural institution.

00:00:11.940 --> 00:00:17.520
But how did the unlikely, raunchy, irreverent
cartoon make it to air in the first place?

00:00:17.520 --> 00:00:19.140
Born of boredom

00:00:19.140 --> 00:00:23.960
Music major Trey Parker and math major Matt
Stone met in film class at the University

00:00:23.960 --> 00:00:26.040
of Colorado in 1992.

00:00:26.040 --> 00:00:30.610
They found themselves stuck on other classmates'
film sets, working the lighting and sound,

00:00:30.610 --> 00:00:33.450
and generally feeling pretty bored.

00:00:33.450 --> 00:00:37.640
Parker and Stone started doing silly voices
to amuse each other and found they had the

00:00:37.640 --> 00:00:39.230
same sense of humor.

00:00:39.230 --> 00:00:40.640
Parker told Entertainment Weekly,

00:00:40.640 --> 00:00:44.340
"We would always talk like these little kids
and make each other laugh."

00:00:44.340 --> 00:00:48.530
They messed around with their characters for
a full year before they ever thought to commit

00:00:48.530 --> 00:00:49.810
anything to film.

00:00:49.810 --> 00:00:52.239
A school presentation

00:00:52.239 --> 00:00:56.670
Before Parker and Stone decided to turn their
characters into pop culture icons, Parker

00:00:56.670 --> 00:01:02.370
made his own cartoon debut with a satirical
school presentation called "American History,"

00:01:02.370 --> 00:01:07.310
which used the same construction paper animation
style that the duo eventually adapted for

00:01:07.310 --> 00:01:08.310
South Park.

00:01:08.310 --> 00:01:12.280
It was the amatuer effort that would launch
a thousand f-bombs.

00:01:12.280 --> 00:01:17.969
The cartoon won a student prize at the University
of Colorado in 1992, inspiring parker and

00:01:17.969 --> 00:01:19.229
Stone to take it further.

00:01:19.229 --> 00:01:23.799
Knowing that the film department had a short
film showcase at the end of the year, they

00:01:23.799 --> 00:01:27.939
decided to do something holiday-appropriate
for their December debut.

00:01:27.939 --> 00:01:33.219
So, they made The Spirit of Christmas, also
known as Jesus vs. Frosty.

00:01:33.220 --> 00:01:34.940
Which is exactly what it sounds like.

00:01:42.149 --> 00:01:47.200
Though they didn't all have names, kids that
looked and sounded pretty similar to Kyle,

00:01:47.200 --> 00:01:49.119
Kenny, Stan, and Cartman are all there.

00:01:49.119 --> 00:01:51.990
It even has some pretty familiar lines.

00:01:51.990 --> 00:01:56.289
"Oh my God, Frosty killed Kenny!"

00:01:56.289 --> 00:01:59.060
At the first screening, the audience went
nuts.

00:01:59.060 --> 00:02:03.540
People were shocked at seeing cute cartoon
kids swear up a blue streak and having Christmas

00:02:03.540 --> 00:02:08.580
icons try to murder one another, but since
it was a college crowd, most of the people

00:02:08.580 --> 00:02:09.580
loved it.

00:02:09.580 --> 00:02:10.580
Cannibal!

00:02:10.580 --> 00:02:11.580
The Musical

00:02:11.580 --> 00:02:16.260
After Jesus vs. Frosty, Parker and Stone didn't
go right to work on more animated shorts.

00:02:16.260 --> 00:02:21.459
Instead, they made a trailer for a musical
comedy about Alferd Packer, a man famous for

00:02:21.459 --> 00:02:25.069
survival cannibalism in Colorado.

00:02:29.760 --> 00:02:34.730
The University of Colorado, which hilariously
named its own cafeteria after Packer, liked

00:02:34.730 --> 00:02:41.560
the trailer so much, that it gave the duo
$125,000 to shoot a full length feature.

00:02:41.560 --> 00:02:44.140
They weren't completely ready to leave snowmen
behind, though.

00:02:45.520 --> 00:02:47.300
"Let's build a snowman."

00:02:52.680 --> 00:02:54.000
The resulting Cannibal!

00:02:54.000 --> 00:02:58.299
The Musical was a glimpse into Parker and
Stone's future, with hints of the demented

00:02:58.299 --> 00:03:02.930
comedy and budding musical genius that would
find its way into their professional projects

00:03:02.930 --> 00:03:04.049
years later.

00:03:04.049 --> 00:03:08.020
And believe it or not, they even hid a few
aliens throughout the film, like they would

00:03:08.020 --> 00:03:09.549
later would in South Park.

00:03:09.549 --> 00:03:14.040
Troma Productions picked up the film for distribution
three years after it was made.

00:03:14.040 --> 00:03:19.390
Brian Graden at the Fox Broadcasting company
happened to see it, and he immediately wanted

00:03:19.390 --> 00:03:20.959
to work with Parker and Stone.

00:03:20.959 --> 00:03:26.129
Though he couldn't get any major studio to
take a liking to Cannibal!, Graden was determined

00:03:26.129 --> 00:03:28.519
to make something happen with the pair.

00:03:28.519 --> 00:03:30.780
The Christmas card and Clooney

00:03:30.780 --> 00:03:34.870
As Graden, Parker, and Stone were trying to
get something off the ground, including a

00:03:34.870 --> 00:03:40.249
failed children's TV show pilot called Time
Warped, Parker and Stone were hurting for

00:03:40.249 --> 00:03:41.249
cash.

00:03:41.249 --> 00:03:47.060
Graden gave them $1,200 to make a video Christmas
card based on the original Jesus vs. Frosty

00:03:47.060 --> 00:03:50.530
short, thinking it would only be seen by a
few of his friends.

00:03:50.530 --> 00:03:54.640
Parker and Stone were excited to get money
to do anything, so they made a new five-minute

00:03:54.640 --> 00:03:59.109
short and figured it would be the last time
they'd visit the quiet little mountain town.

00:03:59.109 --> 00:04:04.650
The Spirit of Christmas, also known as Jesus
vs. Santa, was even more similar to the soon-to-be

00:04:04.650 --> 00:04:06.170
South Park than the original.

00:04:06.170 --> 00:04:10.730
Though the short had many future South Park
staples, Parker and Stone didn't even put

00:04:10.730 --> 00:04:15.480
their names on the film because they figured
it was just a little project for fun.

00:04:15.480 --> 00:04:20.709
Graden ended up sending the tape around to
about 35 friends, but he purposely didn't

00:04:20.709 --> 00:04:24.190
send it to any studio heads, assuming the
material would offend them.

00:04:24.190 --> 00:04:29.700
But the people who actually saw Jesus vs.
Santa loved the short so much, they copied

00:04:29.700 --> 00:04:32.790
it, sent it to friends, and showed it at parties.

00:04:32.790 --> 00:04:36.260
One of the biggest fans of the short was George
Clooney.

00:04:36.260 --> 00:04:40.120
South Park executive producer Anne Garefino
told Hollywood Reporter,

00:04:40.120 --> 00:04:45.160
"Before we even began working on the series,
the fact that George Clooney had made hundreds

00:04:45.160 --> 00:04:50.180
of VHS copies of The Spirit of Christmas and
sent them out to all his friends was already

00:04:50.180 --> 00:04:52.520
the stuff of Hollywood history."

00:04:52.520 --> 00:04:58.150
Without Clooney sharing hundreds of bootleg
VHS tapes, South Park might never have happened.

00:04:58.150 --> 00:04:59.580
Rejection

00:04:59.580 --> 00:05:04.191
Despite other people claiming to have created
the popular, but anonymous, short, Parker

00:05:04.191 --> 00:05:08.890
and Stone eventually got the credit they deserved,
and offers began coming their way…

00:05:08.890 --> 00:05:11.810
including the chance to direct Barney's Great
Adventure.

00:05:11.810 --> 00:05:14.910
Which, obviously, they did not take.

00:05:14.910 --> 00:05:20.010
Graden, Parker, and Stone got meetings everywhere,
but nearly all the studios were afraid of

00:05:20.010 --> 00:05:22.320
putting something so edgy on the air.

00:05:22.320 --> 00:05:27.010
And there was also the assumption that every
show needed to go the Simpsons route and depict

00:05:27.010 --> 00:05:29.410
a family — not a bunch of nasty kids.

00:05:29.410 --> 00:05:35.290
They were turned down by MTV, and Graden was
even turned down by his own network, Fox.

00:05:35.290 --> 00:05:39.630
So, Graden left the company to go wherever
South Park landed.

00:05:39.630 --> 00:05:44.330
Comedy Central they messed afraid of the potty-mouthed
tots and signed them on to make a pilot.

00:05:44.330 --> 00:05:45.580
The crashed pilot

00:05:45.580 --> 00:05:49.880
When Parker and Stone got to bring South Park
to life, they didn't pull any punches.

00:05:49.880 --> 00:05:55.030
The first episode, "Cartman Gets an Anal Probe,"
was shown to focus groups, where people would

00:05:55.030 --> 00:05:57.600
rate the show on a scale of 1 to 10.

00:05:57.600 --> 00:06:00.700
The show hardly got anything over a three.

00:06:00.700 --> 00:06:02.790
And there were lots of ones.

00:06:02.790 --> 00:06:07.100
Three people actually cried because they were
so disturbed over the things the children

00:06:07.100 --> 00:06:08.750
said or did.

00:06:08.750 --> 00:06:11.920
Comedy Central didn't pull the plug on the
series altogether, though.

00:06:11.920 --> 00:06:17.000
They were a struggling network at the time,
and aired a slightly modified pilot mostly

00:06:17.000 --> 00:06:19.660
because they knew it would get them attention.

00:06:27.500 --> 00:06:31.770
South Park debuted in August 1997 after minimal
promotion.

00:06:31.770 --> 00:06:37.300
Graden thought they'd be lucky to get 200,000
people to tune in, but the premiere got 889,000

00:06:37.300 --> 00:06:42.650
viewers, and the show was pulling in over
5 million by the end of the season.

00:06:42.650 --> 00:06:44.750
It was an instant, insane hit.

00:06:44.750 --> 00:06:49.790
Before long, the show was on the cover of
Rolling Stone and Newsweek.

00:06:49.790 --> 00:06:50.890
Cartman eternal

00:06:50.890 --> 00:06:54.920
Despite the remarkable success, Parker and
Stone didn't think they'd be working on it

00:06:54.920 --> 00:06:56.440
for very long at all.

00:06:56.440 --> 00:06:57.960
Parker recalled to The Hollywood Reporter,

00:06:57.960 --> 00:07:00.200
"We thought: 'It's not going to last.

00:07:00.200 --> 00:07:01.200
Take it while you can.'

00:07:01.200 --> 00:07:06.110
We really had the attitude of, 'Let's do this
as long as we can, then we will go back to

00:07:06.110 --> 00:07:07.110
Colorado.'"

00:07:07.110 --> 00:07:12.110
He went on to say that it wasn't until around
season 15 of the show that he realized they'd

00:07:12.110 --> 00:07:14.260
probably never need to go back home.

